    {
      "id": 17,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        1200,
        106
      ],
      "size": [
        440,
        340
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 4,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "GRID. \"rigid\" holds one tempo for the whole track: 121.03 BPM, zero drift.\nUse \"adaptive\" only for music played by hand that genuinely speeds up.\n\nPHASE. Two knobs, different jobs:\n  downbeat_offset     rotates whole beats within the bar (0-3)\n  phase_offset_beats  slides the whole grid, fractions allowed\n\nCuts land on the pickup eighth before each phrase. That is a real edit\nchoice and it works. Set phase_offset_beats to 0.5 to put them on the\ndownbeat instead. Compare by ear: output/beatcheck/phase_00.wav against\nphase_05.wav.\n\nThe report shows the lock score. 1.0 is chance; below 1.6 the grid is not\nlocked and the cuts will look arbitrary."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 18,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        1280,
        582
      ],
      "size": [
        440,
        620
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 5,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "SHOT LENGTH. min_bars applies at peak energy, max_bars in the quiet\nparts, so a chorus cuts fast and a breakdown holds. Lengths snap to 1, 2, 4\nor 8 bars: three-bar shots read as a mistake in 4/4.\n\nCuts always land on bar lines. Lengths are differences between cumulative\nframe positions, so rounding never accumulates and the picture cannot drift\noff the music.\n\nPROMPTS. master_prompt is what must not change. Name the wardrobe\nexplicitly: \"gold sequins\" with no garment named produced sequin-textured\nskin and a nude figure.\n\nframings and lightings take one option per line; blank uses the built-in\nlists. Keep lightings in one colour family or 58 cuts read as strobing."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 19,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        1350,
        1338
      ],
      "size": [
        440,
        300
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 6,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "The image checkpoint makes one start frame per shot. The LTX checkpoint\nanimates them. Two models, two jobs.\n\nLTX needs its text encoder loaded separately: the checkpoint does not carry\none, hence CLIPLoader with type \"ltxv\". The LTX checkpoint's own CLIP\noutput is deliberately unused.\n\nJuggernaut XI at 28 steps, not the Hyper variant at 8. Hyper is four times\nfaster and grows extra torsos on full-body shots."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 20,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        1390,
        1774
      ],
      "size": [
        440,
        500
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 7,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "LIMIT, on both render nodes, and they must match. 8 renders the first\n8 shots in about 3 minutes to check a look. 0 renders the whole song.\n\nCFG ON RENDER SHOTS MUST STAY 1.0. LTX 2B distilled is trained to run\nwithout classifier-free guidance. Raising it degrades the image rather than\nadding control, and with no unconditional pass the negative prompt does\nnothing at all. steps 8 is the intended range, not a compromise.\n\nEach shot is written to disk as it finishes, so a failure at shot 50 does\nnot cost the run. Shots are chained one to the next on purpose: rendering\nin parallel would hold every decoded frame in memory at once."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
